{
  "title": "UK MPs Deny Animals Are Not Recognised as Sentient Beings",
  "date": "2023-11-20",
  "content": {
    "introduction": "A contentious Commons vote last week has stirred significant debate among MPs and animal rights advocates, prompting denials that animals are not acknowledged as sentient beings in UK law.",
    "background": {
      "vote_details": {
        "date": "15 November",
        "result": "313 votes against, 295 votes in favor",
        "issue": "An amendment to incorporate the EU protocol on animal sentience into UK law was defeated."
      },
      "reactions": [
        {
          "name": "Zac Goldsmith",
          "party": "Conservative",
          "statement": "Describing some of the reporting on the vote as 'weird and dishonest.' Goldsmith clarified that the vote was about the legal procedure for animal welfare standards, not the principle of animal sentience."
        },
        {
          "name": "Rachel Maclean",
          "party": "Conservative",
          "statement": "Attempted to address the confusion by posting a video asserting the government's commitment to animal welfare, stating that 'this government... is deeply committed to continuing to protect animals as sentient beings.'"
        },
        {
          "name": "Theresa May",
          "position": "Prime Minister",
          "statement": "Affirmed at PMQs that 'we recognise and respect that animals are sentient beings, and should be treated accordingly.'"
        },
        {
          "name": "Hugo Swire",
          "party": "Conservative",
          "statement": "Shared a letter from Michael Gove, emphasizing that no MP believes animals lack feelings or emotions."
        }
      ]
    },
    "protests": {
      "details": "In the aftermath of the vote, animal rights groups have initiated protests, including several online petitions directed at the House of Lords, UK Parliament, and Michael Gove. Notable public figures, such as presenter Sue Perkins, have publicly criticized the MPs regarding the matter."
    },
    "expert_commentary": {
      "name": "Gudrun Ravetz",
      "position": "Senior Vice-President of the British Veterinary Association",
      "statement": "Expressed deep concern over the vote, indicating that the marginal majority undermines the government's promises to maintain the UK's high standards of animal health and welfare in a post-Brexit era."
    },
    "counter_argument": {
      "name": "Henry Smith",
      "party": "Conservative",
      "statement": "Pointed out that EU law permits various practices that animal rights groups fight against, thus challenging the focus on the amendment."
    },
    "amendment_details": {
      "proposed_by": "Caroline Lucas",
      "party": "Green Party",
      "context": "The amendment sought to integrate article 13 of title II of the Lisbon Treaty into UK law, which explicitly recognises animals as sentient beings since 1999."
    }
  },
  "conclusion": "The debate continues as MPs and public figures clarify their stances on animal sentience, while protests highlight a growing concern over animal welfare in post-Brexit legislation."
}
```

